{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when the ark set forth.</b> He interrupted here with these two verses, and it would have been fitting to join 'And the people were as complainers' with 'when they journeyed from the camp,' because Israel, after journeying from Mount Sinai — which was near to settlement — and going far from it, now coming into the great and terrible wilderness a journey of three days, suffered greatly and complained out of fear of the road, for they said: How shall we be able to bear the suffering and the hardship? If so, since it is all one narrative and one matter, it was not fitting that he should interrupt between them with the section of 'And it came to pass when the ark set forth.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat), the reason for the interruption is, in my opinion, this. Know that the divisions of the lower world are three, and they are: sea, wilderness, and settled land. And so the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'The world — a third of it sea, a third of it wilderness, a third of it settled land.' Now Israel, when they entered the land, passed through these three divisions, and the renewal of the world was demonstrated to them through signs, by those great and awesome wonders which they saw with their own eyes, and through the well-known miracles performed for them in each and every division — the miracles of the sea, the miracles of the wilderness, and the miracles of the conquest of the lands of the seven nations. For the miracles were revealed before the sun. And because those who traverse wildernesses, when they see the high mountains and the mighty rooted rocks, which are the foundations of the earth, the belief in the eternity of the world is reinforced among the naturalists — therefore the wisdom of the Torah was required to set apart this section dealing with the ark, in which are the tablets of testimony, and Moses needed to mention 'And let Your enemies be scattered, and let those who hate You flee before You,' in order to nullify that corrupt belief. For the Torah placed in the ark, together with the scattering of Israel's enemies, is what testifies to the renewal of the world. And therefore great and wondrous miracles were performed for Israel in the wilderness, altering the order of nature, in order to establish in hearts the belief in renewal and to testify to it from within the very place that entails belief in eternity among those who affirm it. From this they enacted for us and said in the Mishnah: 'Over mountains, and over hills, and over wildernesses, one says: Blessed be He who spoke and the world was' — rather, the blessing recited is 'Blessed be the Maker of creation' (עושה בראשית) — to explain that even though it appears to human beings that their existence is eternal and their creation not renewed, everything was created from the beginning, something from nothing, and one who blesses thus testifies to the renewal of the world." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: the Rabbis of blessed memory said in the chapter 'All Holy Scriptures' (Shabbat 115b–116a): this section the Holy One, blessed be He, made signs above and below, to say that this is not its place. Rabbi says: it is not on account of this, but rather it is an independent book. Who disagrees with Rabbi? It is R. Shimon ben Gamliel, as it is taught: R. Shimon ben Gamliel says: this section is destined to be uprooted from its place. And why is it written here? To interrupt between the first punishment and the second punishment. The first punishment: 'And they journeyed from the mountain of YHWH' (Numbers 10:33) — that day they turned away from following YHWH. The second punishment: 'And the people were as complainers' (Numbers 11:1). End quote. <br><b>And there are</b> three opinions on the matter. The opinion of Tanna Kamma: this section should have been written in the section of the standards, in the section of 'In the wilderness of Sinai' (Numbers 2), where it is written (Numbers 2:16–17): 'And the tent of meeting shall journey, the camp of the Levites, in the midst of the camps' — for the ark was in the tent of meeting, and there it would have been fitting to write 'And it came to pass when the ark set forth.' Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, made signs for it, to distinguish it from the sections adjacent to it, before and after, and to teach us that it was written out of its place. And if you count the verses from 'And it came to pass when the ark set forth' until 'And the tent of meeting shall journey,' you will find them to be fifty; and therefore the signs are inverted nuns (נוני\"ן), to teach you that this section turns to the end of fifty sections, which is its place." "<b>And the opinion of</b> Rabbi is that this section was written in its place, for it was written in the context of the journeys. He had already mentioned above at the beginning of the parashah: 'And it came to pass in the second month, on the twentieth day of the month, that the cloud was taken up' (Numbers 10:11), and said: 'And the children of Israel journeyed' (Numbers 10:12), and proceeds to enumerate the journeys of the standards. Therefore he said: 'It is not on account of this,' that is, this is not the reason for the matter — that the signs were because this is not its place — but rather it is an independent book. And therefore the signs were to distinguish it from the sections adjacent to it above and below. By this he meant to say that the Torah, which is the five books of the Torah, is divided into seven books, and the book of Numbers (וידבר) is divided into three books. And they said in the Gemara: according to whom does this follow? According to R. Shmuel bar Nachmani, who said in the name of R. Yonatan (Proverbs 9:1): 'She has hewn out her seven pillars' — these are the seven books of the Torah. According to whom? According to Rabbi. And who disagrees with him? It is R. Shimon ben Gamliel. The opinion of R. Shimon ben Gamliel is that it is in its place, but it is destined to be uprooted from its place in the days of the Messiah, when all punishments will be nullified and the evil inclination abolished, and the reason it was written here was to interrupt between punishment and punishment." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'And it came to pass when the ark set forth' — this section alludes to all the days of the world, for the seven thousand were inscribed in it, as in the first verse, and the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'Six thousand years the world exists, and one is destroyed.' And this matter he wishes to allude to in the context of the wilderness, which is a place of desolation and ruin. And since the Torah is built and founded entirely upon the renewal of the world, one must understand that it has an end, for everything that has a beginning necessarily has an end. And this is what is written (Isaiah 44:6): 'I am the first and I am the last.' And this verse indicates that the world has an end, for just as the Holy One, blessed be He, was first to all creatures, so is He destined to be last to them all; and if any creature remained in the world, He would not be 'last.' And David, peace be upon him, explained this, saying (Psalms 102:26–27): 'Of old You laid the foundation of the earth, and the heavens are the work of Your hands. They shall perish, but You shall endure' — 'and all of them shall grow old like a garment; like a robe You shall change them, and they shall pass away.' And the sixth letter alef is inscribed in the word 'אלפי' to allude to six thousand; and the seventh is sealed in the word 'ישראל,' and the meaning of 'ישראל' here is the removal of the forces, for the cessation of their flow at that time is the seventh thousand, concerning which they said 'and one is destroyed.' And so we find in Scripture (Hosea 9:12): 'בשורי מהם' — with a 'shin' in place of a 'samekh' — from the root meaning removal and nullification. And since the word 'ישראל' includes a thing and its opposite, it is possible that it be interpreted in two ways: one may say that just as it indicates destruction, so it may indicate the creation that comes after destruction, for it includes the dominion of the forces. And this is a hint to you: after the destruction of the seventh thousand, and after the nullification and removal of the forces, dominion will return and the world will be renewed as it was. And this is the secret of the verse (Psalms 24:2): 'ועל נהרות יכוננה' — and it does not say 'כוננה' as it said 'יסדה,' for it spoke of the destruction at the beginning of the psalm — that all will return to the first cause — and it also spoke of the renewal. From this you will find in the psalm 'A song for the Sabbath day,' where it mentions (Psalms 92:13): 'The righteous shall flourish like a palm tree' — an allusion to the existence that comes after the Sabbath. And this is the secret of the conclusion of the section 'And they were finished' (Genesis 2:1–3) with the word 'to do' (לעשות), to allude to doing after the Sabbath, as I have already mentioned there. And this is (Psalms 72:7): 'In his days the righteous shall flourish' — in each sabbatical cycle and sabbatical cycle; 'and abundance of peace, until the moon is no more' — its meaning is that the matter goes thus until the great Jubilee, when all the forces of the world will be nullified, the sun and the moon among them, and the world will return to chaos and void (תהו ובהו). And the verse that says (Genesis 8:22): 'while all the days of the earth remain' — indicates that the earth has a fixed time, and that is the world of the Jubilee. Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, mentioned (Ecclesiastes 1:4): 'and the earth stands forever' — that is the world of the Jubilee, for the matter does not go to infinity. And so too the opinion of his father David, peace be upon him, from what is written (Psalms 102:26–27): 'Of old You laid the foundation of the earth, and the heavens are the work of Your hands. They shall perish, but You shall endure.' And what he said (Psalms 148:2): 'Praise Him, all His angels; praise Him, all His hosts' — 'for He commanded and they were created. And He established them forever and ever' (Psalms 148:5–6). And further he said (Psalms 104:5): 'He founded the earth upon its foundations, that it should not be moved forever and ever' — its meaning is not that He decreed eternity upon them, but rather from the standpoint of the strength of their creation and the power of their material, He said thus, that they are fit to endure forever and not be shaken at all, until He who created them decrees their nullification. And because you should not think that this order and governance of the world's sabbatical cycles goes to infinity, therefore the section is marked with inverted nuns (נוני\"ן) at the beginning and at the end, to explain that it goes until the great Jubilee, which is a thousand generations, and the world will be nullified and return to chaos and void. And the reason the nun is inverted is that just as writing the letter in its proper form indicates the endurance of a thing, so inverting the letter and nullifying it from its proper arrangement indicates the nullification of a thing and its destruction — and therefore the inverted nun. And Scripture says (Job 9:5): 'He removes mountains, and they know not, when He overturns them in His anger.' And it is written (Psalms 46:3): 'Therefore we will not fear, though the earth be transformed, and though the mountains be moved into the heart of the seas' — in the great Jubilee, when the mountains will be shaken and the heavens and the earth will return to their foundation. And in order to strengthen the matter, the inverted nuns were two; and because the heavens and the earth are destined to be renewed after the Jubilee, it is possible to say that therefore there were two inverted nuns. And he mentioned the expression 'ויהי' after each nun and nun, to indicate existence (הויה) after each Jubilee and Jubilee. And this is the secret of the 'nun' that is missing from the psalm titled (Psalms 145) 'A praise of David' — because the letter nun indicates falling (נפילה), and in the Jubilee it is destined to fall and return to chaos and void; and in order to explain that it is destined to be renewed after the Jubilee, he hinted at the 'nun' afterward, saying 'HaShem supports all who fall' (סומך ה' לכל הנופלים) — he mentioned rising after the fall, just as here he mentioned existence after the inverted nuns. And in order to rouse hearts concerning this matter, the commandments of shemittah and Jubilee came in our pure Torah, for they are among the received commandments. And in order that the matter be fixed in your heart and planted in your intellect, I will prove to you what the reason is for the destruction of the world and its renewal in sabbatical cycles, and what the reason is as well for the nullification of existence and its renewal in Jubilees. Know that six sefirot acted during the six days of creation, each sefirah in its day — for example, you may say: the first day, Binah; the second day, Hesed; the third day, Gevurah; and so for all of them — until the seventh day arrived, and it is Yesod, and it performed its action. And what was its action? Pleasure and rest — for there was rest for itself and rest for all six, for by its cessation all of them ceased, and its cessation and rest is its action. And this is (Exodus 20:11): 'For in six days HaShem made the heavens and the earth... and rested on the seventh day' — for He made six sefirot and completed His work through them, and the rest was on the seventh day, which is Yesod. And so the matter is with the six thousand years, for He again began to act in the first thousand with Binah, and in the second with Hesed, and in the third with Gevurah, and so for all of them, until the seventh thousand arrives, which is Yesod, and therefore it is Sabbath and rest, and the world will perforce be destroyed, because each and every sefirah has acted its thousand. The flow ceases to the lower beings when all of them return and ascend on high to receive from Binah, and since Tiferet and Hod, which govern the sun and the moon, ascend on high, the flow must perforce cease — and this is the destruction, that the world will be desolate. But it will not be chaos and void, for they are destined to return as at the beginning and to flow downward what they received above, and then the world will be renewed. And so the matter will be from seven to seven until the great Jubilee, and when the great Jubilee arrives, all ten will return to their root, which is Ein Sof. And this is the reason that the world must perforce become chaos and void, 'and the heavens shall be rolled together like a scroll, and all their host shall fade' (Isaiah 34:4), and 'the earth shall wax old like a garment' (ibid.) — for Tiferet and Hod, corresponding to which are sun and moon, and the heavens and the earth, were absorbed into the holy and returned to their foundation. And this matter was explained to King David in the psalm (Psalms 24:1): 'The earth is YHWH's, and the fullness thereof' — for there he mentioned the return of the supernal and the lower beings to their first foundation, and he began with the lower ones and concluded with the supernal ones. That is what he said at the end, concerning the withdrawal of the measures from flowing below and their ascent and return to Ein Sof (Psalms 24:7): 'Lift up your heads, O gates, and be lifted up, eternal doors' — and the discerning will understand those verses, for I am not permitted to elaborate on their explanation. If so, it necessarily follows that all of existence will be nullified and absent, and after the Jubilee existence will be entirely renewed, for the ten will return as at the beginning, to be revealed from Ein Sof, and the flow will return in the upper and lower beings as in days of old. And so from Jubilee to Jubilee, up to eighteen thousand Jubilees, for each and every sefirah will act a thousand Jubilees, and this corresponds to ten sefirot above and ten sefirot below. And because you should not think that these Jubilees go to infinity and without end, therefore he mentioned 'Return, O YHWH, to the ten thousands' (שובה ה' רבבות), to indicate that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to settle His world for two ten-thousands, which are twenty thousand Jubilees. And he said 'the thousands of Israel' (אלפי ישראל), to explain that He removes two thousands from the ten-thousands. And this is (Psalms 68:18): 'The chariots of Elohim are twenty thousand, even thousands of שנאן' — do not read 'שנאן' but rather 'שאינן,' that is, 'they are not' — and therefore the thousands are not to be mentioned, and thus they are eighteen thousand Jubilees. And this is the secret of the verse (Ezekiel 48): 'round about eighteen thousand.' And so in chapter 141 of Avodah Zarah (3b): if you wish, 'riding upon a cherub, swift and sufficient,' and He passes through His eighteen thousand worlds. From this you learn: 'The chariots of Elohim are twenty thousand...' — do not read 'שנאן' but 'שאינן.' The word 'ישראל' in this context is from the root meaning removal, that He removes two thousand from the two ten-thousands. Hear it and know it for yourself. And behold, this is among the things that are forbidden to us to speculate about, as they said: 'What is above, what is below, what is before, what is after.' And may the Holy One, blessed be He, forgive us and merit us to be counted among those who fear Him and think upon His name, and may He grant us a place among those who stand before Him. And there is yet another reason for the inverted nun: the word 'בנסע' (בנס\"ע) alludes to the numerical value of 'יעקב' (Jacob), and because the figure engraved in the life of the chariot (חיות המרכבה) turns its face toward Him and its back toward the four winds of the world, therefore the inverted nuns came in this section, before it and after it."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "The Mishnah cited (Berakhot 9:5, also Shabbat 9:5) reads 'ברוך עושה בראשית' for wildernesses; the text here appears to conflate the blessing formula 'שאמר והיה העולם' with 'עושה בראשית'. Translated hyper-literally as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:57.746997327Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}